[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one thing: the parser reads dates month first, 412 reports,
one fix queued. That is true of some of the reports. It is not true of all of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. It is two, merged because [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merges on subject line alone, and both read the same to a tired desk at 9am.
</Callout>

## What the table already shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table shows two automatic causes, `parser` and `hint` — but they are one real problem, not
two: nothing in this pipeline checks the tenant's actual locale before reading or hinting a date.
That single defect shows up two ways, by channel. The table's own footnote — reports "read as a
one-day move" — is the tell for the real second problem: a month/day swap moves a date by weeks
or months; these reports move it by exactly one day. That is not this bug at all.

## The two problems

1. **Date reading isn't locale-aware.** On `app`/`email`/`booking-v2`-`form` traffic, the shared
   parser reads a correctly-typed date as `MM/DD` regardless of locale, flipping it for day-first
   tenants (`parser`, 379 reports). On `booking-v3`-`form` traffic, the form's hint tells those
   same tenants to type `MM/DD/YYYY`, and the form then reads what they typed the tenant's actual
   day-first way — flipping it for the opposite reason (`hint`, 33 reports). Same defect, two
   symptoms. **412 reports** — the full ticket, counted as the desk's own cause split already has
   it. → [issue #1](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
2. **The one-day bug.** Five of those same 412 — flagged by `desk-notes.json` and footnoted on
   the table above — are off by exactly one day, not a month/day swap: a meeting that showed one
   date in Lisbon and another in Toronto, a month where every booking ran a day late and only
   that month, an invite that arrived before the booking it was for. They stay filed under
   `parser` above (that's where `causeOf()` puts them), but the actual cause is a timezone or
   day-boundary problem, unrelated to date-order at all. **5 reports**, a subset of the 412, not
   an addition to it. → [issue #3](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3)
   (issue #2 was opened and then folded into #1 once it was clear it was the same defect, not a
   second one — see the comment on #1.)

## Why the desk didn't see it

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is right that merging on subject is fast
and that splitting later is cheaper than two engineers chasing the same bug. It says nothing
about the other direction: two different bugs can also *share* a subject line, and merging them
hides that there were ever two. "Merge when unsure" needs a partner rule: **before you mark a
big ticket known, check whether every report in it actually shares a cause, not just a
subject.** `causes.ts` already has the logic to check this; #4471 just never ran it before
someone wrote the word "known" on the page.

## Lessons

- A ticket this size is not "mostly one bug reported many times" by default — it is one bug
  only if something actually checked.
- A footnote on a table is a finding somebody has to read and act on, not decoration. This one
  sat there since the table was built.
- The desk's own tool for telling causes apart (`causes.ts`) already existed. Nobody pointed it
  at #4471 until now.

